{
  "index": 18,
  "response": " This problem is a classic example of the \"Henneart's problem,\" also known as the \"Fox, Chicken, and Corn\" riddle. Here's the optimal solution:\n\n1. The farmer takes the grain across the river.\n2. He returns to the original side and picks up the chicken.\n3. He crosses the river again, leaving the chicken on the other side.\n4. He goes back one more time to get the fox and brings it across the river.\n5. The farmer leaves the fox on the opposite bank (since the chicken is there, the fox won't eat the chicken).\n6. The farmer makes one final trip to retrieve the chicken from the other side.\n7. Finally, he crosses the river one last time with the chicken, completing the journey and ensuring that neither the fox nor the chicken eats the grain during the process.\n\nThis sequence ensures that all three items are safely transported across the river without any incidents involving the predator (fox) or prey (chicken).",
